526 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( The focus of this report is the O2C process, which includes order placement, payment, and delivery. This report analyzes the current workflow of the process \(AS-IS\), identifies problems, and proposes an improved version \(TO-BE\). Both quantitative and qualitative methods are used in the analysis, which is backed by simulation tools and business process modeling with BPMN. The redesign aims to increase productivity, adaptability, and service quality, while keeping expenses reasonable.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
527 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( By implementing these suggestions, Home Burger will be able to manage the growing demand better and keep a competitive advantage in the evolving food delivery industry.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
528 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 369 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
529 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 369 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
530 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Background)
/E (Background)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
531 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Despite its success, the company operates in an increasingly competitive market. The surge in delivery services after COVID-19 and evolving customer expectations have created pressure to improve operational efficiency. Each store currently manages its orders and customer data independently, often leading to delays, errors, and resource constraints.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 370 0 R
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
535 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 370 0 R
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
536 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Initial Assumptions)
/E (Initial Assumptions)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
537 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In the initial phase, several assumptions were necessary to facilitate a detailed analysis and process modeling due to undefined parameters in the project description.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Firstly, we assumed specific salary figures for roles essential to the process, such as the manager, as these were not provided in the project documentation. For example, salaries for various roles, including phone operators, kitchen staff, couriers, and reception employees, were derived from general cost information given. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Secondly, where exact process times were not specified, we established average completion times based on either analogous tasks in the process or reasonable estimates drawn from industry benchmarks. For example, the average time for customer interactions during phone and in-person orders was standardized based on the provided task durations for related processes. This step was taken to ensured the consistency required to simulate and evaluate the current \(AS-IS\) and redesigned \(TO-BE\) processes.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Lastly, we assumed that all tasks follow a normal truncated distribution to reflect realistic variability while avoiding extreme outliers. Additionally, start events, such as incoming customer orders, were modeled using a Poisson distribution to capture the random and time-dependent nature of these occurrences.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
