816 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The company registered a total of 5250 orders. Out of these, 52 percent were placed through the call center, amounting to 2730 orders. On average, this translates to 91 orders per day registered at the call center over a 30-day period.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
817 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (However, only 70 percent of calls to the call center are answered. To calculate the total number of calls received, divide the average daily orders by the call answer rate:)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
818 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Total calls received per day = 91 / 0.7 = 130)
/E (Total calls received per day = 91 / 0.7 = 130)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The start event of the call process follows a Poisson distribution. Given an 8-hour workday \(equivalent to 28800 seconds\), the mean arrival rate \(lambda\) is calculated as:)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean arrival rate = 130 / 28800 = 0.00451 calls per second)
/E (Mean arrival rate = 130 / 28800 = 0.00451 calls per second)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The mean inter-arrival time, representing the average time between two consecutive calls, is the reciprocal of lambda:)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00451 = 221.73 seconds)
/E (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00451 = 221.73 seconds)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (This means, on average, a call is received every 221.73 seconds.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
