1206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In Scenario 3, with two employees and a 90% call pickup rate, the average processing time for the Order-to-Cash \(O2C\) process increased to 253.48 seconds \(4 minutes and 13 seconds\), compared to 198.85 seconds \(3 minutes and 19 seconds\) in the AS-IS scenario. This increase of 54.63 seconds reflects the additional load of calls being handled due to the higher pickup rate.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Key observations include:)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 6459 0 R
/ActualText (Waiting Time Introduced: The system now experiences an average waiting time of 26.35 seconds, indicating that some calls are placed on hold before being attended to. For instance:)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 6460 0 R
/ActualText ("Enter customer phone number into the orders IS" saw a waiting time of 16.64 seconds.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ("Transfer the call to the right store" experienced a waiting time of 11.12 seconds.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 6461 0 R
/ActualText (Increased Utilization: Employee utilization rose to 57.75%, which is a significant increase compared to the AS-IS scenario, but still manageable without overburdening the staff.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (While this configuration reduces the chance of missed calls and increases the system's ability to handle more requests, it introduces delays in processing and waiting times, making it less efficient overall. The trade-off between a higher call pickup rate and increased processing time suggests that further optimization may be required. Balancing employee resources with call pickup rates is essential for achieving better efficiency and maintaining customer satisfaction.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
