1530 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In this scenario, the maximum customer arrival count through the website remained at 68, similar to Scenario 1, but the number of website employees was increased to 2. The results showed:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1531 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 6736 0 R
/ActualText (The average process time stayed consistent at 193.46 seconds \(approximately 3.22 minutes\), the same as in Scenario 1. This indicates that increasing the number of employees did not impact the time taken to process individual orders.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 6737 0 R
/ActualText (The total process time also remained at 13,155.54 seconds \(approximately 219.26 minutes\), as the workload distribution across two employees was smooth and efficient.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The utilization rate for each employee decreased significantly to 38.63%, compared to 77.26% in Scenario 1. This reduction in utilization highlights the improved workload distribution and reduced pressure on individual employees.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (With no waiting times recorded, this setup ensures uninterrupted task execution, effectively handling the increased order volume. This configuration demonstrates an efficient balance between workload and employee capacity, providing flexibility to manage further increases in demand or handle unexpected delays without compromising performance.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1535 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 402 0 R
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1536 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 402 0 R
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1537 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Orders registered at the store)
/E (Orders registered at the store)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The company registered a total of 5250 orders, of which 22.5% were placed in person at the store, accounting for 1181 orders. On average, this translates to approximately 39 orders per day registered in the physical store over a 30-day period.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The store operates with one employee responsible for registering the in-person orders. Assuming the employee works for 8 hours per day, this is equivalent to 28,800 seconds \(8 hours x 60 minutes x 60 seconds\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (To calculate the mean arrival rate \(lambda\), we divide the total daily orders by the total number of seconds the employee works per day:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1541 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean arrival rate \(lambda\) = 39 orders / 28,800 s = 0.00135 orders per second.)
/E (Mean arrival rate \(lambda\) = 39 orders / 28,800 s = 0.00135 orders per second.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1542 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The mean inter-arrival time, representing the average time between two consecutive orders, is the reciprocal of lambda: )
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1543 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00135 = 740.74 seconds.)
/E (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00135 = 740.74 seconds.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1544 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (This means, on average, an in-person order is placed every 740.74 seconds \(approximately 12.68 minutes\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
