1830 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The company registered a total of 5250 orders. Since all orders must go through the order processing system, this accounts for 100 percent of the total orders. On average, this translates to 175 orders per day processed by the system over a 30-day period.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1831 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The start event of the order processing workflow follows a Poisson distribution. Given an 8-hour workday \(equivalent to 28800 seconds\), the mean arrival rate \(lambda\) is calculated as:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1832 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean arrival rate = 175 / 28800 = 0.00608 orders per second.)
/E (Mean arrival rate = 175 / 28800 = 0.00608 orders per second.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1833 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The mean inter-arrival time, representing the average time between two consecutive orders being processed, is the reciprocal of lambda:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1834 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00608 = 164.47 seconds.)
/E (Mean inter-arrival time = 1 / 0.00608 = 164.47 seconds.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1835 0 obj
<<
