/S /P
/ActualText ( Key observations:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7718 0 R
/ActualText (Average time increased from 123.26 seconds \(2.1 minutes\) in Scenario 3 to 146.45 seconds \(2.4 minutes\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
2639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7719 0 R
/ActualText (Average waiting time rose from 82.99 seconds to 149.34 seconds, indicating minor inefficiencies.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 7720 0 R
/ActualText (Waiting time for Items Received and Left for Delivery increased from 37.19 seconds in Scenario 3 to 53.45 seconds.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Resource utilization rates showed mixed results. The packager utilization increased to 82.44 percent, while the courier utilization remained stable at 82.66 percent, indicating balanced workloads. The receptionist utilization increased slightly to 91.60 percent, reflecting their additional responsibilities. Kitchen staff utilization rose marginally to 77.58 percent, maintaining a manageable workload.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (This scenario effectively eliminates the manager role, reducing costs. However, the redistribution of tasks caused minor inefficiencies, particularly in packaging and delivery tasks. This highlights the trade-off between cost savings and maintaining process efficiency.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
